# Conventions — TypeScript style

## Context

The app is TypeScript strict (`noUncheckedIndexedAccess` on). Consistency keeps
the reducer/actions/selectors trio predictable. See `app/src/actions.ts` and
`app/src/reducer.ts` for the established style.

## Rule

- Use NAMED exports only. NEVER use `export default`.
- NEVER use `any`, `as any`, `@ts-ignore`, or `@ts-expect-error` to silence the
  type checker. Fix the types instead.
- Update state IMMUTABLY: build new objects/arrays with spread / `map` / `filter`;
  never mutate `state`, `state.tasks`, or a task in place.
- Name source files in kebab-case (e.g. `text-utils.ts`); use PascalCase for
  types/interfaces and camelCase for functions/variables.
- Import types with `import type { ... }` and keep the `.js` extension in
  relative import specifiers (ESM), matching existing files.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rn "export default" app/src` returns zero matches.
2. `grep -rEn "\bany\b|@ts-ignore|@ts-expect-error" app/src` returns zero matches.
3. `cd app && npm run typecheck` passes with no errors.
4. Reducer cases return new objects (no `state.x =` assignments).


